{% comment %}
  Customer purchase limits: one row per product, and one row is the whole change.

  Each row takes three values:

    limit_handle   EasyStore product handle or SKU. Matched against both, because
                   order line items expose one or the other depending on the
                   store, and normalized to lowercase at runtime because
                   EasyStore product URLs are lowercase even when the
                   administrative value is capitalized.
    limit_maximum  units one customer may buy. Delete the row to leave a product
                   unlimited; handles that are not listed here are unlimited.
    limit_refresh  the date the allowance is counted from, so a limit is measured
                   over a window instead of over every order ever placed. Orders
                   placed before it stop counting and every customer starts that
                   limit again from zero. A timestamp in the future changes
                   nothing until it arrives. Write the store's timezone offset:
                   '2026-09-01 00:00:00 +0800'.

  A blank limit_refresh falls back to customer_order_limit_refresh_all below,
  which renews every limit that does not name its own date. Every row leaves its
  own blank today, so all of them count from that shared date. When both are
  blank a limit counts every past order instead.

  To renew one limit, set its limit_refresh rather than clearing its maximum:
  clearing the maximum disables the limit entirely, while a refresh keeps it
  enforced for purchases made from that date onwards.

  A family of products that shares a handle prefix takes one prefix row, of the
  form written at the bottom of this file, instead of one row per product. Every
  product whose handle starts with prefix_handle is limited to prefix_maximum
  units, each product counted on its own, so a product added to the family later
  is limited without a configuration change. Delete the prefix row to leave the
  whole family unlimited.
{% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
  Every limit counts from 9 Aug 2026, store time: orders placed before it no
  longer consume an allowance. A row that needs a different date sets its own
  limit_refresh, which overrides this.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign customer_order_limit_refresh_all = '2026-08-09 00:00:00 +0800' %}
